---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import { href, siteStats } from '../../lib/catalog';
const stats = siteStats();
---
<Layout title="Scans & signing — reading the grype results" description="How each published digest is scanned with grype, why cosign signing is gated on zero Low+ findings, and how to read the counts.">
  <section>
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href={href('/docs/')}>Docs</a></p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Scans &amp; signing</h1>
      <p class="section-sub">Every count on this site is a raw grype finding against the exact digest that was published.</p>

      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-top:36px">What is scanned</h2>
      <p>
        After publish, each image's multi-arch manifest is scanned with the latest
        <a href="https://github.com/anchore/grype" rel="noopener">grype</a>. The full JSON report is kept with
        the build entry in <a href={href('/data/index.json')}>data/index.json</a> — the Advisories tab on each image
        page is rendered from it. No VEX statements or ignore rules are applied.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-top:36px">The signing gate</h2>
      <p>
        An image is cosign-signed only when its scan reports <b>zero findings at Low severity or above</b>.
        Negligible and Unknown findings do not block signing. Currently {stats.signed} of {stats.images} images
        carry a signature on their latest build.
      </p>
      <p>
        An unsigned image is still published and usable — the gate exists so that a signature means
        something specific: <i>this digest scanned clean when it was built</i>. Use
        <code class="kv-mono">cosign verify</code> in admission control (Kyverno, policy-controller) to only admit
        images that passed it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-top:36px">Reading the counts</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Fixable</b> means grype knows a fixed version. Most fixable findings disappear on the next daily rebuild once Wolfi ships the fix.</li>
        <li><b>Not fixed / won't fix</b> findings usually sit in a bundled runtime (e.g. Chromium in the Grafana renderer) and clear when that package moves upstream.</li>
        <li>A <b>clean</b> scan means no findings in one database snapshot — not proof the image is free of vulnerabilities.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
